From 4b687854d2deabbfa8095a581c9cb8f78446a1a5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: mesa-nvk-horizon <mesa-nvk-horizon@localhost>
Date: Sun, 23 Aug 2026 21:51:39 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 20/55] nouveau/vk: nvkmd_horizon queue, submit, fences, waits
 and destruction
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Milestone items 6 to 10. Every operation nvkmd defines is now
implemented; no entry point in this backend returns "not implemented"
any more.

Decision D11, native over syncpoints. A binary vk_sync backed by a
channel fence — a syncpoint id and a threshold — with the runtime's
vk_sync_timeline emulation on top for timeline semaphores, which is what
NVK does on every backend whose kernel driver has no timeline of its
own. The emulation needs a binary type underneath regardless; the only
question was whether that should be a syncpoint fence or a CPU-only
event, and a syncpoint fence is what a submit actually produces. The
emulated timeline type is registered in the pdev's sync list — NVK
advertises KHR_timeline_semaphore unconditionally, so vkCreateSemaphore
must find one — and the type declares GPU_MULTI_WAIT alongside GPU_WAIT,
which vk_sync_timeline requires of the type it is built on and which
nvkmd_horizon_ctx_wait, taking a wait_count, already does.

The type also carries the timeline bookkeeping nvk_mem_stream needs:
nvk_mem_stream_init creates its stream sync with VK_SYNC_IS_TIMELINE,
and vk_sync_get_value calls sync->type->get_value with no NULL check —
without it, chunk recycling was a jump to address zero. The object
tracks the value its pending fence will complete and the largest value
known to have completed; get_value polls the fence with a zero timeout
(which also notices a faulted channel) and ctx_signal passes
signal_value through rather than dropping it. Binary use is unchanged.

A wait may arrive before there is a fence (decision D16, raised by
review on PR #6): this type advertises CPU_WAIT and CPU_SIGNAL together,
so another thread may signal or submit while the first is inside
vkWaitForFences, and Vulkan requires that wait to block until the
timeout rather than report one that has not happened. The state is
shared properly rather than sampled: a mutex and a condition variable in
the sync, broadcast from signal(), set_fence() and move(). move()
preserves the destination's live mutex and condvar across the payload
copy and locks the two objects in address order; the condvar wait is
chunked at 100 ms because cnd_timedwait's TIME_UTC deadline is the
real-time clock on this platform (decision D8). The primitives are
verified on this platform: t_threads passes 67/67 on console.

Decision D8's practical consequence, handled in one place. vk_sync waits
take an absolute deadline built from os_time_get_nano(), and on this
platform that clock was measured to be the real-time clock — t_ostime
saw TIME_MONOTONIC return epoch seconds. The deadline is therefore
converted to a relative duration exactly once, at the moment the wait
starts, and horizon_gpu waits with libnx's tick-based sleep, which is
monotonic in hardware. A date change during the wait cannot move the
deadline because by then there is no deadline left to move.

Submission is one submit per exec, one span array sized to exec_count.
horizon_gpu's only working submit mode, HORIZON_GPU_SUBMIT_DEFAULT, is
already NOT_MAIN | NO_PREFETCH — the other value is the R3 experiment
that failed on hardware — so nvkmd's per-entry no_prefetch flag selects
nothing, and horizon_gpu_submit takes any number of spans and reports
BUSY on a full ring rather than truncating. This retires "extension 2"
of the six Phase 4 step 1 enumerated: per-span submit flags are not
needed, because there is only one flag combination that works.

A push is refused, not asserted, in all three ways it can truncate: a
byte count that is not a whole number of dwords, an address with its low
two bits set, and a length at or above 2^21 dwords — a GPFIFO entry
carries a dword-aligned address and a 21-bit dword count (envytools
gf100+ entry format), and each narrowing is silent at submit time and a
channel fault much later. The nouveau backend asserts the same values;
here they go straight into an entry whose fields just truncate.

Where a CPU stall happens, and where it does not:

  exec()     never. The submit is asynchronous and stays that way.
  sync()     always, and that is what it is for: vkQueueWaitIdle and
             vkDeviceWaitIdle are made of it. No deadline, because
             those two entry points have none; horizon_gpu implements
             that as a bounded re-checking loop rather than an infinite
             kernel wait, and re-checks the error notifier, so a
             faulted channel ends it.
  wait()     on the CPU for now, and stated rather than hidden. A
             GPU-side wait is what this should be — the channel
             stalling on the syncpoint — and the methods that do it
             need a command-stream builder this backend does not have
             yet. It runs only when the application asked one
             submission to wait for another, never after every submit.
  flush()    nothing to flush: horizon_gpu_submit appends and kicks off
             in the same call.

A bind is performed immediately rather than queued: Horizon has no
VM_BIND queue, NVGPU_AS_IOCTL_MAP_BUFFER_EX is synchronous, and the
mapping is in place before the call returns. That is stricter than the
interface requires, not looser.

A signal with nothing submitted issues a fence-only submit rather than
signalling on the CPU: the signal must still be ordered after any
earlier work on the channel, and only the channel can say that.

`engines` is not a channel property here. Horizon binds engines by
emitting SET_OBJECT into the stream, and horizon_gpu_channel_bind_engines
does all five subchannels at once with the device's queried class
numbers — there is no channel that can do 3D but not compute. It is
submitted asynchronously; the next submit is ordered after it by the
channel itself.

mesa-nvk-horizon: Phase 4 items 6-10
Why: nvkmd's submit and sync contract is written against DRM syncobjs
  and a validating kernel; on Horizon the fence is a raw syncpoint
  threshold and a GPFIFO entry's fields truncate silently, so the
  backend must supply itself the checks, the registration and the
  timeline bookkeeping the nouveau backend gets from the kernel and
  the syncobj.
Evidence: libnvk.a is 94 812 bytes with all seven backend objects and 0
  TLS relocations, and no VK_ERROR_FEATURE_NOT_PRESENT milestone stub
  remains in nvkmd/horizon/. t_threads passes 67/67 on console for the
  condvar primitives the wait path relies on.
Upstream: no — this is the Horizon backend's own queue and sync path.

Absorbs (2026-08-23 series compaction; full history in mesa-patches/'s
own git log):

- 0022 "register the emulated timeline sync type": a missing pdev list
  entry that would have failed every vkCreateSemaphore(timeline).
- 0024 "refuse a push that is not a whole number of dwords".
- 0026 "one submit per exec, not a run of them": the run-splitting
  bought nothing (only one working submit mode) and cost a stack
  overflow — seventeen consecutive `incomplete` entries wrote past the
  fixed 16-element run array and the n == 16 flush test never matched
  again.
- 0027 "declare GPU_MULTI_WAIT on the sync type":
  vk_sync_timeline.c:51-60 asserts it of the type it is built on.
- 0032 "check all three ways a push truncates": envytools gf100+ GPFIFO
  entry format; nvkmd_nouveau_ctx.c asserts the same values.
- 0036 "the sync type nvk_mem_stream uses needs get_value": get_value
  was NULL and vk_sync_get_value has no NULL check.
- 0044 "a wait may arrive before there is a fence": decision D16, PR #6
  review; t_threads 67/67 on console.
